            'excerpt' => 'Skills-first hiring works when employers replace weak pedigree filters with stronger evidence of capability, outcomes and role-specific judgement.',
            'content' => <<<'HTML'
<p><strong>Skills-first hiring is not simply removing degree requirements.</strong> It means changing the evidence used to make a hiring decision. Employers move away from treating pedigree, job titles or company brands as proof of capability and ask a harder question: what has this person actually demonstrated, and is that evidence relevant to this role?</p>

<p>The shift is gathering momentum. OECD research published in 2025 and 2026 describes skills-first practices as a growing labour-market approach, while LinkedIn's 2025 recruiting research found that 93% of talent-acquisition professionals considered accurate skills assessment crucial to quality of hire.</p>

<h2>What does skills-first hiring actually mean?</h2>
<p>It means defining the capabilities required for the job and assessing candidates against those capabilities using evidence. Education and previous employers still provide context, but they stop functioning as automatic proxies for competence.</p>

<p>For a commercial leader, the evidence may include P&amp;L ownership, channel expansion, pricing decisions and margin improvement. For a plant leader, it may include safety, quality, throughput, cost and workforce outcomes. For a technology leader, it may include architecture choices, delivery reliability, team scale and product impact.</p>

<h2>Why is pedigree alone a weak hiring signal?</h2>
<p>Job titles are inconsistent across companies. A vice president in one organisation may have less scope than a general manager in another. Employer brands can also disguise the difference between participating in a successful system and personally driving the result.</p>

<p>A skills-first process forces the interviewer to unpack what happened: What was the baseline? What did the candidate own? What changed? What constraints existed? Which decisions were theirs? What was the measurable or observable outcome?</p>

<h2>Should employers stop looking at degrees and experience?</h2>
<p>No. The objective is not to throw away useful context. It is to prevent proxies from becoming shortcuts that eliminate relevant candidates too early. OECD analysis notes that work experience and degrees remain widely used in hiring even as employers expand skills tests, pre-employment assessment and other methods.</p>

<p>For regulated, technical or specialised roles, formal qualifications may still be essential. Skills-first hiring means being precise about when a credential is genuinely required and when it is simply inherited from an old job description.</p>

<h2>How should employers assess skills in senior hiring?</h2>
<p>Senior hiring requires more than a technical checklist. The assessment should examine the scale and complexity at which the capability was demonstrated. A leader who managed a function of 20 people in one market is not automatically equivalent to a leader managing 500 people across several regions.</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Define the outcome:</strong> what must improve in the first 12 to 24 months?</li>
<li><strong>Identify evidence:</strong> what achievements would prove the candidate has solved a comparable problem?</li>
<li><strong>Test depth:</strong> ask how the result was produced, not just what the result was.</li>
<li><strong>Calibrate scale:</strong> compare team size, revenue, geography, complexity, regulation and decision rights.</li>
<li><strong>Assess transferability:</strong> decide whether the candidate's capability can work in your operating context.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Can AI make skills-first hiring easier?</h2>
<p>Yes, particularly at the discovery stage. AI can help extract skills from profiles, identify adjacent experience and search beyond conventional titles. It can also help recruiters compare evidence more consistently. But employers still need a reliable assessment process because a listed skill is not the same as demonstrated proficiency.</p>

<p>That distinction matters more as AI makes it easier for candidates to improve the presentation of CVs and profiles. Hiring teams need to move from keyword recognition to evidence validation.</p>

<h2>What mistakes make skills-first hiring fail?</h2>
<p>The most common mistake is relabelling the same old process. If a company says it is skills-first but still shortlists only by employer brand, degree, exact title and years of experience, nothing has changed.</p>

<p>A second mistake is using generic assessments that are disconnected from the actual role. A good assessment should measure capabilities that matter to performance, not simply generate more scores.</p>

<p>A third is ignoring context. Two candidates can have the same nominal skill while having applied it at dramatically different levels of complexity.</p>

<h2>What does HiredNext recommend?</h2>
<p>Start the search by defining evidence of performance before looking at names. Then map the market broadly enough to include adjacent talent pools and assess candidates against the mandate, not against a preconceived profile.</p>

<p>HiredNext discussed the shift toward skills and AI in <a href="https://etedge-insights.com/industry/hr/indias-recruiters-explore-new-approaches-as-skills-and-ai-shape-hiring-decisions/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ET Edge Insights</a>. The same principle applies across executive search and mid-senior hiring: fewer assumptions, better evidence.</p>

