            'content' => <<<'HTML'
<p><strong>The annual campus drive is no longer enough for many employers.</strong> Hiring demand changes too quickly, skills evolve too fast and business teams rarely create vacancies on the same academic calendar. That is pushing recruitment toward a more continuous model: always-on talent discovery.</p>

<p>HiredNext contributed to this discussion in <a href="https://www.financialexpress.com/jobs-career/the-fall-of-the-campus-hiring-and-the-rise-of-always-on-recruitment-4113576/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Financial Express</a>, which examined the move away from treating campus recruitment as a single seasonal event.</p>

<h2>What is always-on recruitment?</h2>
<p>Always-on recruitment means maintaining an active view of talent even when there is no immediate vacancy. Employers track emerging skills, build relationships with relevant candidates, maintain warm talent pools and start market mapping before a hiring requirement becomes urgent.</p>

<p>It does not mean interviewing people constantly. It means reducing the amount of time an organisation spends starting from zero.</p>

<h2>Why is the traditional campus model under pressure?</h2>
<p>The traditional model assumes companies can predict headcount far in advance, hire a large cohort at one time and rely on academic credentials as a sufficiently strong early filter. That works in some high-volume environments, but it is less effective when roles are changing quickly or require specialised skills.</p>

<p>The World Economic Forum's Future of Jobs Report 2025 found that employers expect a large share of job skills to change by 2030, with AI, data and cybersecurity among the fastest-growing skill areas. When capability requirements move faster than curriculum cycles, employers need more flexible ways to identify talent.</p>

<h2>Does always-on hiring mean campus hiring is dead?</h2>
<p>No. Campuses remain an important source of early-career talent. The change is that employers are increasingly combining campus relationships with internships, apprenticeships, project evidence, skills assessments, digital communities and off-cycle hiring.</p>

<p>The best campus strategy may therefore look less like one large annual transaction and more like a year-round relationship with selected institutions and talent pools.</p>

<h2>How does skills-first hiring change graduate recruitment?</h2>
<p>It gives employers more signals than college name and degree. Projects, internships, portfolios, competitions, certifications, technical assessments and demonstrated problem-solving can help identify candidates who may otherwise be missed.</p>

<p>For employers, this creates a broader pool. It also raises the bar for assessment: if pedigree matters less, the organisation needs a better way to determine who can actually perform.</p>

<h2>Where can AI support always-on recruitment?</h2>
<p>AI can help maintain and search larger talent pools, identify changes in candidate profiles, match emerging roles to previously discovered candidates and automate low-value follow-up. It can also make it easier for recruiters to search by skills rather than exact titles.</p>

<p>But continuous recruiting should not become continuous spam. Relevance matters. A candidate is more likely to engage when outreach reflects their actual experience and explains why the opportunity fits.</p>

<h2>What should employers build before moving to an always-on model?</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>A skills taxonomy that is usable:</strong> not hundreds of vague labels, but clear capability groups linked to real roles.</li>
<li><strong>Priority talent pools:</strong> focus on roles where lead time, scarcity or business impact justifies proactive mapping.</li>
<li><strong>Candidate consent and communication discipline:</strong> maintain useful relationships rather than collecting inactive databases.</li>
<li><strong>Recruiter ownership:</strong> technology can surface possibilities, but someone must judge relevance and manage the relationship.</li>
<li><strong>Conversion metrics:</strong> measure whether warm pools actually improve speed, quality or acceptance when roles open.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What is the advantage for business leaders?</h2>
<p>Urgent hiring becomes less reactive. When a new product line, plant, region or capability is approved, the recruitment team already understands the market and may already know relevant people.</p>

<p>That is especially valuable for leadership and specialist hiring, where the strongest candidates are often not actively applying at the moment the vacancy appears.</p>

<h2>What is the practical takeaway?</h2>
<p>Campus hiring still matters, but employers should stop treating talent acquisition as a calendar event. Build market intelligence continuously, assess skills more directly and maintain relationships with talent before a requisition becomes critical.</p>

