            'excerpt' => 'A strong CV can earn attention, but hiring decisions are made on relevance, evidence, risk and role fit. Impressive experience is not always the same as a convincing match.',
            'content' => <<<'HTML'
<p><strong>A strong CV is not the same as a strong match.</strong> Candidates are often surprised when an impressive career does not convert into an interview or offer. The reason is usually not that the experience has no value. It is that hiring decisions are comparative: employers are deciding which candidate presents the clearest evidence for this specific problem, at this specific moment.</p>

<h2>Why can an impressive CV still be rejected?</h2>
<p>Because recruiters do not evaluate careers in the abstract. They evaluate relevance. A candidate may have worked for excellent companies and delivered strong outcomes, but if the CV does not make the connection to the role obvious, another candidate can appear safer or more immediately useful.</p>

<p>At senior levels, the question becomes even more specific: has this person handled comparable scale, complexity, stakeholders and business consequences?</p>

<h2>Does a recruiter read every CV in detail?</h2>
<p>Not at the first stage. Initial review is usually about identifying enough evidence to justify deeper attention. That means the top section of a CV, role chronology, scope and achievements need to communicate relevance quickly.</p>

<p>This is one reason generic CVs underperform. A document that tries to be suitable for every role often fails to make a strong case for any particular role.</p>

<h2>What evidence do recruiters look for?</h2>
<p>Recruiters look for proof that the candidate has done work comparable to what the employer needs. Strong evidence is specific enough to understand scope and outcome.</p>

<ul>
<li>Size of business, portfolio, team or geography.</li>
<li>Nature of the problem or mandate.</li>
<li>What the candidate personally owned.</li>
<li>What changed because of their decisions.</li>
<li>Relevant constraints such as regulation, cost, turnaround pressure or scale.</li>
</ul>

<p>“Responsible for growth” is weak. Explaining the market, baseline, decisions and outcome gives the recruiter something to evaluate.</p>

<h2>Can too much information hurt a CV?</h2>
<p>Yes. Senior candidates often mistake completeness for clarity. A CV that records every responsibility from a 20-year career can bury the evidence that matters most.</p>

<p>The purpose of the document is not to preserve the entire career history. It is to make the candidate's value legible. Detail should be proportional to relevance.</p>

<h2>Why do candidates get rejected after a good first interview?</h2>
<p>Because interviews change the evidence set. The CV creates an expectation; the conversation tests it. Candidates can lose momentum when achievements remain vague, examples are difficult to verify, motivations sound generic or the level of personal ownership is unclear.</p>

<p>Sometimes the candidate performs well and still loses to someone whose experience is closer to the mandate. Rejection is not always evidence of poor performance.</p>

<h2>How important is motivation?</h2>
<p>Very. Employers want to know not only whether a candidate can do the job, but whether the person genuinely wants this job. At senior levels, weak motivation creates offer risk and joining risk.</p>

<p>A convincing answer connects the next move to the candidate's career logic: scope, challenge, team, geography, industry, transformation opportunity or long-term trajectory.</p>

<h2>Does ATS optimisation guarantee a shortlist?</h2>
<p>No. Keywords can help a CV become discoverable, but they cannot substitute for evidence. Over-optimised documents can also become repetitive and unnatural.</p>

<p>The better objective is accurate language: use terminology the target market understands, make relevant skills explicit and show where those skills produced outcomes.</p>

<h2>What should candidates change first?</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Choose a target:</strong> know the role family and seniority you are positioning for.</li>
<li><strong>Lead with relevance:</strong> put the strongest evidence for that target near the top.</li>
<li><strong>Quantify selectively:</strong> use numbers where they clarify scale or impact, not as decoration.</li>
<li><strong>Remove weak repetition:</strong> responsibilities that do not differentiate you can be shortened.</li>
<li><strong>Prepare the story behind each claim:</strong> interviews will test the evidence in the CV.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Can a recruiter-led CV assessment help?</h2>
<p>It can help identify where the document is making the candidate harder to understand: unclear positioning, buried achievements, weak evidence, inconsistent seniority signals or missing role-relevant language. It cannot guarantee an interview because the hiring decision remains with the employer.</p>

<p>HiredNext offers <a href="https://hirednext.net/services/cv-assessment">CV assessment</a> and broader <a href="https://hirednext.net/services/candidates">candidate career services</a> for professionals who want recruiter-informed feedback on positioning and interview readiness.</p>

<h2>What is the most important takeaway?</h2>
<p>Do not ask whether your CV is impressive. Ask whether it gives a recruiter enough relevant evidence to choose you over other plausible candidates for the target role. Clarity beats volume, and evidence beats adjectives.</p>
